According to section 26WF Exception—remedial action, if:

- An access to, or disclosure of, information is covered by paragraph 26WE(2)(a);
- The APP entity, credit reporting body, credit provider or file number recipient, as the case may be, takes action in relation to the access or disclosure;
- The action occurs before the access or disclosure results in serious harm to any individual to whom the information relates; and
- As a result of the action, a reasonable person would conclude that the access or disclosure would not be likely to result in serious harm to any such individuals;

then:

- This Part does not require:
  (e) the APP entity, credit reporting body, credit provider or file number recipient, as the case may be;
  (f) any other entity;  
  to take steps to notify the individual of the contents of a statement that relates to the access or disclosure.

This means that if an entity takes remedial action before serious harm is likely and concludes it would not occur, they are exempt from notifying individuals about the details of the breach.

Yes, remedial action taken after unauthorised access or disclosure following a loss of information can prevent the loss from being an eligible data breach. According to s 26WF Exception—remedial action (3), if:
- The loss is covered by paragraph 26WE(2)(b);
- The APP entity, credit reporting body, credit provider or file number recipient takes action in relation to the loss;
- The action occurs before there is unauthorised access to, or unauthorised disclosure of, the information; and
- As a result of the action, a reasonable person would conclude that the access or disclosure would not be likely to result in serious harm to any of those individuals;

then the loss is not an eligible data breach. This means that if the entity takes appropriate steps before the unauthorised access or disclosure occurs, it can prevent the loss from being classified as an eligible data breach under s 26WE Eligible data breach.

According to s 26WG of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), relevant matters include:

(c) the kind or kinds of information;
(d) the sensitivity of the information;
(e) whether the information is protected by one or more security measures;
(f) if the information is protected by one or more security measures—the likelihood that any of those security measures could be overcome;
(g) the persons, or the kinds of persons, who have obtained, or who could obtain, the information;
(h) if a security technology or methodology:
(i) was used in relation to the information; and
(ii) was designed to make the information unintelligible or meaningless to persons who are not authorised to obtain the information;
the likelihood that the persons, or the kinds of persons, who:
(iii) have obtained, or who could obtain, the information; and
(iv) have, or are likely to have, the intention of causing harm to any of the individuals to whom the information relates;

These matters must be considered when determining whether a reasonable person would conclude that an access or disclosure would result in serious harm.

According to section 26WK of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), the statement about an eligible data breach must include:

(a) the identity and contact details of the entity;
(b) a description of the eligible data breach that the entity has reasonable grounds to believe has happened; 
(c) the particular kind or kinds of information concerned; 
(d) recommendations about the steps that individuals should take in response to the eligible data breach that the entity has reasonable grounds to believe has happened.

A secrecy provision for the purposes of the exception to eligible data breach statement and notification obligations, as per section 26WP of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), means a provision that:
(a) is a provision of a law of the Commonwealth (other than this Act);
(b) prohibits or regulates the use or disclosure of information.

The Commissioner's notice must set out either or both of the following:
(i) action the entity must take, or refrain from taking, within a reasonable period specified in the notice, to address the contravention;
(ii) steps the entity must take, within a reasonable period specified in the notice, to ensure that the conduct constituting the contravention is not repeated or continued.

According to section 26X Eligible Data Breach Declaration, the following things may be specified as a permitted purpose in relation to an eligible data breach:

(a) preventing or reducing a risk of harm mentioned in paragraph (1)(b) to one or more individuals at risk from the eligible data breach.

(b) responding to a cyber security incident, fraud, scam activity or identity theft;

(c) responding to the consequences of a cyber security incident, fraud, scam activity, identity crime and misuse, financial loss, emotional and psychological harm, family violence and physical harm or intimidation; 

(d) addressing malicious cyber activity.
